\description{
Ten summary statistics of a pairwise genome alignment that were not designed
to represent a biological fact.  They were retained from a screen of 5,420
functions assembled mechanically from the columns of the alignment table
and from generic descriptive statistics ("monkey typing"), for the following
properties on 1,910 alignments of 16 clades: reproducible between the
alignment of \emph{A} to \emph{B} and the alignment of \emph{B} to \emph{A}, more alike between
genome pairs that share an ancestor than between random pairs, different
from the value obtained after shuffling the order and the strand of the
alignments, and not strongly correlated with the statistics that already
existed in this package, nor with each other (absolute rank correlation
below 0.6).  Their biological meaning, if any, is to be found.  Each function
is named after a famous monkey.
}
\details{
All the functions take the alignments as loaded, without coalescing them, and
sort them on the \emph{target} genome.  They use the following quantities.
\itemize{
\item A \emph{junction} is a pair of consecutive alignments on the same \emph{target}
sequence.  A \emph{same-query junction} is a junction whose two alignments are
also on the same \emph{query} sequence.
\item The \emph{target gap} of a junction is the number of bases between its two
alignments on the \emph{target} genome, plus one; the \emph{query gap} of a
same-query junction is the same on the \emph{query} genome, whatever the order
and the strand of the two alignments.
\item The \emph{gap contrast} of a same-query junction is \eqn{(t - q) / (t + q)}
where \eqn{t} and \eqn{q} are its target gap and its query gap.  It is
positive when the two alignments are further apart on the \emph{target}.
\item The \emph{rank step} of a same-query junction is the number of positions
between its two alignments in the order of the alignments on the \emph{query}
genome (1 for neighbours), and its \emph{direction} is +1 when the two
alignments are on the same strand and follow each other on the \emph{query}
as that strand implies, -1 when they follow each other in the opposite
direction, and 0 when they are on opposite strands.
\item The \emph{edge distance} of an alignment on a genome is the distance from its
centre to the nearest end of the sequence, divided by half the length of
the sequence: 0 at the ends, 1 in the middle.  The \emph{relative position} is
the position of the centre from 0 (first base) to 1 (last base).
\item \emph{Sizes} (widths, gaps, rank steps) are used on the logarithmic scale.
}

The functions are the following.  The monkey is introduced first.

\describe{
\item{\code{wukong_index()}}{\emph{Sun Wukong: the Monkey King of the Chinese novel
Journey to the West.}  Tail weight of the query gaps: on the logarithmic
scale, range between the 10th and the 90th percentiles divided by the
interquartile range.}
\item{\code{abu_index()}}{\emph{Abu: kleptomaniac pet monkey of the street thief in
the Aladdin films.}  Quartile skewness of the gap contrasts:
\eqn{(Q_3 + Q_1 - 2 Q_2) / (Q_3 - Q_1)}.}
\item{\code{albert_index()}}{\emph{Albert II: rhesus macaque, first primate to reach
space, in 1949.}  Excess kurtosis of the gap contrasts.}
\item{\code{marcel_index()}}{\emph{Marcel: white-headed capuchin kept by Ross in the
sitcom Friends.}  Standard deviation of the absolute difference between
the relative positions of an alignment on the two genomes.  The \emph{query}
position is counted from the other end of the sequence when most
aligned bases between the two sequences are on the minus strand.}
\item{\code{imo_index()}}{\emph{Imo: Japanese macaque of Koshima who started washing
sweet potatoes.}  Correlation between the edge distance of an alignment
on the \emph{target} genome and the edge distance of its neighbours on the
\emph{query} genome.  Neighbours are the alignments before and after it on
the same \emph{target} sequence; the two members of a junction are
exchangeable.}
\item{\code{rafiki_index()}}{\emph{Rafiki: mandrill shaman who presents the newborn
cub in The Lion King.}  Rank correlation, over same-query junctions,
between the query gap and the mean edge distance of the two alignments
on the \emph{query} genome.}
\item{\code{hanuman_index()}}{\emph{Hanuman: monkey-faced Hindu deity, devoted
companion of Rama in the Ramayana.}  Rank correlation, over junctions,
between the mean width of the two alignments and the absolute difference
of their edge distances on the \emph{query} genome.}
\item{\code{jack_index()}}{\emph{Jack: chacma baboon who operated railway signals in
South Africa in the 1880s.}  Rank correlation, over same-query junctions,
between the rank step and the direction.}
\item{\code{george_index()}}{\emph{Curious George: inquisitive little monkey of the
children's books by the Reys.}  Correlation, over junctions, between the
target gap and the mean score per aligned column of the two alignments.
Stops with an error without the \code{score} and \code{aLength} metadata
columns.}
\item{\code{baker_index()}}{\emph{Miss Baker: squirrel monkey who survived a
suborbital spaceflight in 1959.}  Correlation, weighted by the width of
the alignments on the \emph{target} genome, between the number of gap
openings per aligned column of an alignment and the geometric mean of
the target gaps of the junctions that flank it.  Stops with an error
without the \code{gapOpens} and \code{aLength} metadata columns.}
}

Ranks are computed within each vector before restricting it to the
junctions used: in \code{rafiki_index()}, the mean edge distances are ranked
among all junctions.  Variances are population variances.  Quantiles are
those of \code{\link[stats:quantile]{stats::quantile}} with default settings.

The results do not depend on the order of the sequences nor on their
orientation: reversing a sequence with \code{\link{reverse}} does not change them.
They are not symmetric: the value on \code{swap(gb)} differs.  Note that
\code{\link{swap}} drops the metadata columns that \code{george_index()} and
\code{baker_index()} need.

The functions that use edge distances or relative positions need sequence
lengths.  Missing lengths are replaced by the end of the last alignment of
each sequence, with \code{\link{forceSeqLengths}}.
}
\examples{
# Alignment of two yeast genomes
gb <- load_genomic_breaks(
  system.file("extdata/SacCer3__SacPar.gff3.gz", package = "GenomicBreaks"))
wukong_index(gb)
abu_index(gb)
albert_index(gb)
